---
/**
 * hospitality / WhyBK — "WHY-US" (Villa Marindu / BK).
 *
 * ROLE: the book-direct block. The eyebrow, one Prata headline, and beneath it
 * a three-track row of BRASS-TOPPED COLUMNS — each a 2px brass rule, a serif
 * brass numeral, the reason's title and the owner's paragraph. It is the only
 * section in the template that argues rather than describes, which is why it
 * carries no photograph and no ledger: three short columns of reasoning, exactly
 * as "Villa Marindu Landing.dc.html" draws them.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every metric below is lifted verbatim from
 * that file's data-component="WHY-US": grid repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)) / gap 44px,
 * column border-top 2px brass + padding-top 22px, numeral Prata 30px brass
 * line-height 1, title Prata 24px with margin 14px 0 10px, body 15px/1.75 in
 * --grey, and an h2 with margin .26em 0 48px at max-width 26ch. Nothing here is
 * re-derived to survive an admin colour scheme (R1): the colours are the
 * wrapper's tokens whose VALUES are this design's hex — --accent (#B99552),
 * --bone-hi (#F4F1EA), --grey (#A9AFA9). No literal hex appears in this file.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts
 * under the "Why us" group, so v3's sidebar owns a real input for each — an
 * undeclared path would be a dead hook that focuses nothing):
 *   why.tag                — the eyebrow, the wrapper's .kick primitive
 *   why.headline           — the h2, rendered with set:html so an owner's own
 *                            <em> picks up PageBK's italic-brass emphasis (the
 *                            template's signature). That rule is GLOBAL in the
 *                            wrapper on purpose: scoped styles cannot reach
 *                            elements created by set:html (R3), so an italic
 *                            rule written in this file would never apply.
 *   why.items.${i}.title   — the reason (h3)
 *   why.items.${i}.body    — the reason's paragraph. CANONICAL key: the value
 *                            can arrive as .description through
 *                            astro-builder's normalizeBlock alias map (and as
 *                            .desc from older payloads), so the READ tolerates
 *                            all three while the data-field always spells the
 *                            key the schema declares — otherwise the sidebar
 *                            never receives the edit.
 *
 * R2 — NOTHING IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE. Not one visible string is written
 * here. The design's demo copy ("Why book direct", "Three reasons guests skip
 * the platforms", "One price, said once") is the DESIGNER filling in their own
 * template; a real villa gets a page that says only what that villa said. Every
 * guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so an empty value removes the ELEMENT: no titled
 * heading over nothing, no empty paragraph, and — because the 2px brass rule
 * belongs to the COLUMN — no brass rule floating over a row that is not there.
 *
 * DERIVED, not authored:
 *   · THE NUMERAL. The design's demo data carries an `n` field ('01','02','03');
 *     our schema has no numeral input, so the numeral is computed from the
 *     DISPLAY index, zero-padded to two digits, and marked aria-hidden — it is
 *     ordinal decoration, and a screen reader already gets the order from the
 *     document. It is the ONE thing keyed to the display index rather than the
 *     original one (R6): after a blank row is dropped the printed sequence must
 *     read 01·02·03, never 01·03. Everything an EDIT touches uses the ORIGINAL
 *     index, so deleting reason 2 can never misdirect a write into reason 3.
 *   · The brass top rule and the column frame. Decorative, no data-field.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED (so none of it reads as an oversight):
 *   · why.image and why.lead. Both are schema-declared and both are used by the
 *     BJ sibling, but this design's WHY-US has neither a plate nor a lead
 *     paragraph — it is eyebrow, headline, three columns. Adding either would be
 *     building a different section than the one that was handed over.
 *   · NO id ON THE SECTION. The design gives WHY-US none, and PageBK's
 *     ANCHOR_ALIAS deliberately remaps the derived '#why' nav href onto
 *     '#about'. An id here would create a target the navigation never points at.
 *   · NO onPage() ANCHOR HELPER (R7). This section renders no href of any kind —
 *     no button, no link — so there is nothing to remap. The helper is absent
 *     rather than shipped unused; if a link is ever added here it must be copied
 *     in from the sibling with PAGE_ANCHORS = about/services/gallery/location/
 *     faq/main, exactly as the other BK sections carry it.
 *   · NO PER-REASON ICON. A drawn pictogram claims WHAT the reason is about; the
 *     owner's words are the only signal this section is allowed to carry.
 *
 * TRACK COUNT vs ITEM COUNT: the grid is fixed at three tracks because the
 * design fixes three tracks. An owner who adds a fourth reason gets a second
 * row, not four squeezed columns — the design's proportions survive, and no
 * owner content is refused.
 *
 * SECTION PADDING is the wrapper's, not this file's. The design writes
 * `max-width:1280px;margin:0 auto;padding:100px 34px 0`: the 1280/gutter half is
 * the shared `.wrap`, and the 100px top is `html[data-page="BK"] section`'s
 * clamp(60px,8vw,100px), which caps at exactly that. The design's bottom-0 is a
 * WHOLE-PAGE rhythm decision (every unbanded section in it pads top only) and it
 * lives in the wrapper's shared rule; one section zeroing its own bottom while
 * its fourteen siblings keep theirs is the kind of local re-derivation that put
 * the previous template out of step with its design. Flagged, not done here.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBK passes it to every section uniformly;
// this block reads nothing outside content.*.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const why = content.why || {};
const tag = typeof why.tag === 'string' ? why.tag.trim() : '';
// Not trimmed into a plain string operation beyond trim(): this goes out through
// set:html, so whatever markup the owner typed reaches the wrapper's em rule.
const headline = typeof why.headline === 'string' ? why.headline.trim() : '';

type Item = { title?: string; body?: string; desc?: string; description?: string };
const items: Item[] = Array.isArray(why.items) ? why.items : [];
const titleOf = (it: Item): string => String(it?.title ?? '').trim();
const bodyOf = (it: Item): string => String(it?.body ?? it?.desc ?? it?.description ?? '').trim();

// A reason the owner added but never filled would otherwise spend a brass rule,
// 22px of padding and a numeral on nothing. Drop the blanks (whitespace counts
// as blank) but carry the ORIGINAL index `i` so every data-field still writes to
// the real array slot.
const rows = items
  .map((it, i: number) => ({ i, title: titleOf(it), body: bodyOf(it) }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.title || r.body));

// SECTION GATE (R5). Counts the COLUMNS only. lib/derive-content-defaults.ts
// emits why.tag ('Why us') and why.headline ('Why choose X') for every site
// while shipping items: [] — so a gate that counted the tag or the headline
// would be always-true and would ship an eyebrow and a headline over an empty
// three-track grid on every first build.
const hasReasons = rows.length > 0;
---
{hasReasons && (
  <section class="reasons">
    <div class="wrap">
      {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="why.tag">{tag}</span>}
      {headline && <h2 class="h2 reasons-h reveal" data-field="why.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
      <div class="reasons-grid">
        {rows.map((r, d: number) => (
          /* `d` is the DISPLAY index and `r.i` is the ORIGINAL one. The numeral
             is the only thing keyed to `d`, so the printed sequence stays
             unbroken after a blank row is dropped; it is aria-hidden because the
             order is already carried by the document. The stagger tops out at
             the wrapper's last declared delay, so a fourth reason simply reveals
             with the third. */
          <div class="reasons-col reveal" data-delay={d > 0 ? String(Math.min(d, 3)) : undefined}>
            <div class="fig reasons-n" aria-hidden="true">{String(d + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</div>
            {r.title && <h3 class="reasons-t" data-field={`why.items.${r.i}.title`}>{r.title}</h3>}
            {r.body && <p class="reasons-p" data-field={`why.items.${r.i}.body`}>{r.body}</p>}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* ── the headline. The wrapper's .h2 primitive already carries the design's
        face, clamp(31px,4vw,54px), line-height 1.1 and --bone-hi; only the two
        values this section states differently are restated — the design's
        `margin:.26em 0 48px` and its 26ch measure (the shared primitive caps at
        24ch). Both of PageBK's primitives are :where()-wrapped, so a scoped
        class here out-specifies them without an !important. */
  .reasons-h { margin: .26em 0 48px; max-width: 26ch; }

  /* An owner who clears the headline is a real case, and then the eyebrow would
     sit flush on the first brass rule — the h2's own margins are what normally
     open that gap. Adjacency, so it fires ONLY in that case. */
  .kick + .reasons-grid { margin-top: 34px; }

  /* ── the three brass-topped columns. Tracks and gap exactly as drawn. */
  .reasons-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 44px;
  }
  /* min-width:0 as well as the minmax() above: a grid item's default min-width
     is auto, and one unbroken word in a title would otherwise widen its track
     and push the page sideways. */
  .reasons-col {
    min-width: 0;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--accent);
    padding-top: 22px;
  }

  /* The numeral rides the wrapper's .fig primitive (Prata, --accent, tabular
     lining figures, line-height 1); only the design's size is stated here. */
  .reasons-n { font-size: 30px; }

  /* Prata ships ONE weight (400); asking for a bolder one makes the browser
     synthesise a faux bold, so the weight is pinned rather than inherited from
     whatever a heading rule elsewhere asks for. line-height is left to the
     wrapper's shared heading rule on purpose — it is part of the template's
     voice, and the design states no line-height of its own here. */
  .reasons-t {
    font-family: var(--disp); font-weight: 400; font-size: 24px;
    margin: 14px 0 10px; color: var(--bone-hi);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* PageBK's `:where(p){margin:0 0 1em}` sits at (0,1,1), so this paragraph rule
     is qualified with its parent class to out-specify it — the design's
     paragraph has no bottom margin at all. */
  .reasons-col .reasons-p {
    margin: 0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75; color: var(--grey);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── 900 · the collapse this family gives a three-track row (HowBK's grid is
        the same construct in the same design, and CredentialsBK's rail matches):
        two tracks here, one at 560. Two-up leaves each column ~400px — still a
        column, and it keeps this section in step with HOW-IT-WORKS two sections
        below, which would otherwise be 2-up at the width this one went 1-up. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .reasons-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 36px; }
    .reasons-h { margin-bottom: 36px; }
  }

  /* ── 760 · the wrapper's shared pass releases :where(.h2)'s measure here. The
        26ch above is a scoped class and out-specifies it, so it has to be
        released on the same line or the headline keeps a 26ch strip on every
        phone while its siblings run full width. */
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .reasons-h { max-width: none; }
  }

  /* ── 560 · one column. minmax(0,…) rather than 1fr: a single track still
        defaults to an auto minimum. The paragraph takes the measure cap here,
        where it is the full width of the wrap rather than a column. */
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .reasons-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 30px; }
    .reasons-col .reasons-p { max-width: 62ch; }
  }

  /* ── 520: tighten the stack. Nothing here may push the page sideways, which
        the overflow-wrap above and the minmax/min-width tracks already cover. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .reasons-grid { gap: 26px; }
    .reasons-h { margin-bottom: 28px; }
    .kick + .reasons-grid { margin-top: 24px; }
    .reasons-n { font-size: 26px; }
    .reasons-t { font-size: 21px; }
  }
</style>
